data.content:
Here are the three breaking changes and their fixes:

## 1. `Buffer.cs` — `[InlineArray]` on `record struct` banned in C# 13

Record structs synthesize methods (e.g. `Equals`, `ToString`) that access all fields, which conflicts with inline-array semantics. Change to a plain `struct`:

```csharp
[System.Runtime.CompilerServices.InlineArray(10)]
struct Buffer
{
    private int _element0;
}
```

## 2. `Generator.cs` — `stackalloc` in local function inside iterator

C# 13 now allows `unsafe` contexts in iterators, but as a side-effect, local functions inside iterators are treated as part of the iterator — making `stackalloc` illegal there. Fix by extracting to a regular static method:

```csharp
unsafe class DataGenerator
{
    IEnumerable<int> Generate()
    {
        yield return 1;
        Process();
    }

    static void Process()
    {
        int* p = stackalloc int[10];
        // ... work with pointer
    }
}
```

## 3. `Resolver.cs` — `Process([])` now ambiguous

C# 13's expanded `params` support and collection-expression changes mean `[]` is now convertible to both `ReadOnlySpan<int>` and `Span<object>`, causing ambiguity. Fix by specifying the target type explicitly:

```csharp
static void Main()
{
    Process(ReadOnlySpan<int>.Empty);
    // or: Process((ReadOnlySpan<int>)[]);
}
```
